<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="course-info">
  <title>General Course Information</title>

  <introduction>
    <figure xml:id="fig-topics">
      <caption>A tour of MATH 14: curves and vector fields, double and
      triple integrals, surfaces and flux, and the bridges <mdash/> the big
      theorems <mdash/> that connect them.</caption>
      <image source="calculus-adventure.png" width="92%">
        <shortdescription>A hiker on a floating island crossing bridges past a vector field, a polar grid, a curved surface, and a dome of flux arrows toward an observatory</shortdescription>
        <description>
          <p>
            A colorful cartoon of a floating island built from the ideas of
            this course. A hiker with a backpack and a walking stick stands
            at the lower left, gesturing at the scene. A glowing golden path
            <mdash/> a parametrized curve <mdash/> winds from the hiker's
            feet across the whole island. Beside the hiker, a meadow is
            covered in small blue arrows, a vector field, and a lake sits
            inside a glowing closed loop, the boundary curve of a line
            integral. Farther along, a flat region is ruled into a
            rectangular grid on one side and a polar grid of circles and
            rays on the other, the regions of double integrals. Small golden
            bridges carry the path over gaps in the island, the theorems
            that connect one kind of integral to the next. To the right, a
            rainbow-colored curved surface is drawn with a grid and arrows
            flowing across it, a surface integral, and a transparent dome
            bristles with arrows pointing outward through it, the flux of a
            vector field out of a closed surface. Waterfalls spill off the
            island's edges. The path climbs to a summit where an observatory
            tower and a large glass globe stand, the destination at the end
            of the quarter. Every one of these ideas is listed in
            <xref ref="specific-goals" text="title"/>.
          </p>
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      <dl>
        <li>
          <title>Course</title>
          <p>MATH 14 <mdash/> Calculus and Analytic Geometry IV</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Instructor</title>
          <p>Mehdi Ahmadi. I prefer to go by my first name.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Office</title>
          <p>St. Joseph's Hall 111</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Email</title>
          <p><email>mahmadi@scu.edu</email></p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Lecture dates</title>
          <p>
            September 21 <ndash/> December 4, 2026. The final examination period is
            December 7 <ndash/> 11, 2026.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Prerequisite</title>
          <p>
            MATH 13 or equivalent. A grade of C<ndash/> or higher in MATH 13 is
            strongly recommended before taking MATH 14.
          </p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </p>

    <p>
      This syllabus applies to section 14-5 of MATH 14, whose meeting time and
      room are listed in <xref ref="table-sections"/>.
    </p>

